e1=15, html4=19, space1=20, append6=26, space1=27, 插1=28, 入1=29, 含1=30, space1=31, option6=37, space1=38, 的1=39, space1=40, HTML4=44, space1=45, 可1=46, space1=47, XSS3=50, ，1=51, 升1=52, space1=53, 3.5.0 5=58, 。1=59. Good. Use this. It doesn't mention <option> tags, but \"option\" enough? Maybe better \"含 <option>\" but adds 2. 61. Could omit \"的\": \"jQuery 3.4.1 用 html/append 插入含 <option> HTML 可 XSS，升 3.5.0。\" Count: jQuery6+1+5+1+用1+1+html4+1+append6+1+插1入1含1+1+<option>8+1+HTML4+1+可1+1+XSS3+，1+升1+1+3.5.0 5+。1 = 6+1=7+5=12+1=13+1=14+1=15+4=19+1=20+6=26+1=27+1=28+1=29+1=30+1=31+8=39+1=40+4=44+1=45+1=46+1=47+3=50+1=51+1=52+1=53+5=58+1=59. Good. Use \"含 <option> HTML\" maybe grammatically okay. Summary: \"jQuery 3.4.1 用 html/append 插入含 <option> HTML 可 XSS，升 3.5.0。\" It's 59 by count. Good.\n\nBut maybe \"html/append\" should be `.html()/.append()`? That adds 4 chars. Could still be <=60? Let's count with `.html()/.append()`: jQuery6+1+5+1+用1+1+.html()/.append()17+1+插1入1含1+1+<option>8+1+HTML4+1+可1+1+XSS3+，1+升1+1+3.5.0 5+。1 = 6+1=7+5=12+1=13+1=14+1=15+17=32+1=33+1=34+1=35+1=36+1=37+8=45+1=46+4=50+1=51+1=52+1=53+3=56+1=57+1=58+1=59+5=64+1=65. Too long. So use html/append.\n\nNeed ensure JSON string with `<option>` is okay. Yes.\n\nexploit_evidence: [] because no search results. Could there be evidence from vulnerability record? It says \"may execute untrusted code\" not actual exploitation or PoC. No search results. So [].\n\nfixed_version: \"3.5.0\".\n\nNow final JSON:\n{\n  \"vulnerable_symbols\": [\"$.fn.html\", \"$.fn.append\"],\n  \"trigger\": \"将包含 <option> 的不可信 HTML 传入 jQuery 的 .html()/.append() 等 DOM 操作方法。\",\n  \"preconditions\": [],\n  \"exploit_evidence\": [],\n  \"fixed_version\": \"3.5.0\",\n  \"summary_zh\": \"jQuery 3.4.1 用 html/append 插入含 <option> HTML 可 XSS，升 3.5.0。\"\n}\n\nDouble-check JSON valid: quotes, no trailing commas.\n\nPotential concern: `vulnerable_symbols` should be application code entry, not internal prototype. Maybe `[\"jQuery.html\", \"jQuery.append\"]` is more understandable. But `$.fn.html` is precise. If the package is npm/jquery, the global `$` may not be available in Node? But application code uses `jQuery` or `$`. The methods are on `jQuery.fn`. I think okay.\n\nCould include `[\"jQuery.fn.html\", \"jQuery.fn.append\"]` to avoid `$` ambiguity. Which is better? The examples use library namespace: `_.template`, `yaml.load`. For jQuery, namespace is `jQuery`. The method is `jQuery.fn.html`. But application code calls `jQuery(selector).html()`. The API symbol could be `jQuery.fn.html`. I'll use `[\"jQuery.fn.html\", \"jQuery.fn.append\"]` maybe clearer. But `$` is common. The field says API, not code snippet. `jQuery.fn.html` is the actual function. Let's choose `[\"jQuery.fn.html\", \"jQuery.fn.append\"]`.\n\nTrigger: \"将包含 <option> 的不可信 HTML 传入 jQuery 的 .html()/.append() 等 DOM 操作方法。\" Good.\n\nPreconditions: Should we include \"HTML 中包含 <option> 元素\"? It's in trigger. No extra.\n\nBut maybe the vulnerability requires \"HTML 经过 sanitizer 但 sanitizer 允许 <option>\"? The record says even after sanitizing them. But trigger can be untrusted HTML containing option. No config.\n\nLet's consider if `preconditions` should include \"目标元素不是 <select>\"? If we omit, maybe card less precise. But if we include and it's not strictly required, could be wrong. Let's verify exact condition from jQuery 3.5.0 release notes. I can recall: \"jQuery 3.5.0: Security: XSS via HTML containing <option> elements. This problem is patched in jQuery 3.5.0. To workaround, use DOMPurify with SAFE_FOR_JQUERY option.\" The blog likely says: \"When jQuery parses HTML strings, it uses a temporary container. If the string contains <option> elements, it wraps the string in <select> to ensure proper parsing. However, if the resulting nodes are inserted into a context other than <select>, the <option> elements may be parsed as HTML, allowing XSS.\" If so, precondition: inserted into non-select context. But the record doesn't mention. Should we infer? The user says based on vulnerability record and search results. Search results none. We should not over-infer beyond record? But as security analyst, we can use known. However, if we include a precondition not in record, might be okay if true. Need decide.\n\nLet's try to find exact mechanism from memory. jQuery 3.5.0 commit: \"Fix XSS via HTML containing <option> elements\". The fix likely in `buildFragment`: when the HTML contains `<option>`, it now uses a `<select>` container only if the context is a select? Or it escapes? Let's recall jQuery issue #4650? The fix: \"Do not allow <option> elements to be inserted into a non-select context\"? Maybe they changed `buildFragment` to not wrap in select unless the context is select? Let's search memory: In jQuery 3.5.0, `buildFragment` had:\n```\n// Support: IE 9 - 11+\n// IE's #replaceWith is buggy\nif ( !context.createElement ) {\n  ...\n}\n// Support: iOS 7 only\n// In iOS 7, `option` shorthand\nif ( html.indexOf(\"<option\") > -1 ) {\n  div.innerHTML = \"<select>\" + html + \"</select>\";\n  // Support: IE 9\n  if ( !div.firstChild ) {\n    throw new Error( \"jQuery: HTML string is invalid\" );\n  }\n  // Support: IE 9 - 11+\n  // IE's #replaceWith is buggy\n  div = div.firstChild;\n}\n```\nThe fix might be to check `context` and if not select, use `div.innerHTML = html`? But that would still parse option in body context, maybe XSS? Hmm.\n\nLet's look at actual vulnerability: If jQuery wraps in select, option content is text. If it doesn't wrap, option in body context may be ignored and content parsed as HTML, causing XSS. So the bug might be that jQuery wraps in select, but then when inserting into non-select, the option element's `innerHTML` is text, no XSS. Wait, if it doesn't wrap, XSS. But jQuery did wrap to fix IE option shorthand? Maybe the wrapping introduced XSS? Let's test: If jQuery wraps in select, no XSS. If it doesn't, XSS. But the vulnerability exists in 3.4.1, so maybe 3.4.1 does not wrap? Or wraps but still XSS due to another reason.\n\nLet's recall jQuery's `buildFragment` special handling for `<option>` was added for IE 9? It wraps in select. If that wrapping is present, how XSS? Maybe because after wrapping, it sets `div.innerHTML = \"<select>\" + html + \"</select>\"`, then `div = div.firstChild` (select). Then it gets `div.childNodes`. If the HTML is `<option><img src=x onerror=alert(1)>`, in select context, the option's content is text. But then when jQuery appends the option node to a non-select element, the browser may re-parse the option's text as HTML? No, DOM nodes are not re-parsed. Unless jQuery serializes the fragment back to HTML and sets innerHTML again? In `domManip`, for HTML strings, it calls `buildFragment` and then appends nodes. No serialization.\n\nMaybe the XSS is when the HTML string contains `<option>` and is passed to `.html()` on a `<select>` element. jQuery's `html()` method for select might use `option` shorthand? Let's inspect `jQuery.fn.html`:\n```\nhtml: function( value ) {\n  return access( this, function( value ) {\n    ...\n    if ( isFunction( value ) ) { ... }\n    // Build up a new list of nodes to be inserted\n    var elem = this[ 0 ];\n    if ( elem ) {\n      // Remove everything in the selected elements\n      jQuery( elem ).empty();\n      if ( typeof value === \"string\" ) {\n        value = jQuery.htmlPrefilter( value );\n        // If the type is a number and it is a string, it is a number\n        if ( rhtml.test( value ) ) {\n          // Ensure that the string is not empty\n          if ( rhtmlNode.test( value ) ) {\n            // If the type is a number and it is a string, it is a number\n            value = jQuery.parseHTML( value, this[0].ownerDocument, true );\n          }\n        }\n        jQuery( elem ).append( value );\n      }\n    }\n  }, null, value, arguments.length );\n}\n```\nIt calls append. `jQuery.parseHTML` uses buildFragment.\n\nMaybe the XSS is via `jQuery.parseHTML` returning a document fragment where `<option>` elements have their `innerHTML` set to the raw string? Let's check `parseHTML`:\n```\nparseHTML: function( data, context, script ) {\n  if ( typeof data !== \"string\" ) { return []; }\n  if ( typeof context === \"boolean\" ) { script = context; context = false; }\n  context = context || document;\n  var parsed = rsingleTag.exec( data ),\n    wrap = ...,\n    ...\n  if ( parsed ) {\n    return [ context.createElement( parsed[1] ) ];\n  }\n  parsed = buildFragment( [ data ], context, script );\n  ...\n}\n```\n`buildFragment` for HTML string:\n```\nfunction buildFragment( elems, context, scripts ) {\n  var elem, tmp, tag, wrap, attached, j,\n    i = 0,\n    l = elems.length,\n    ...\n  // If the type is a number and it is a string, it is a number\n  if ( context === document ) {\n    ...\n  }\n  // If the type is a number and it is a string, it is a number\n  for ( ; i < l; i++ ) {\n    elem = elems[ i ];\n    if ( elem || elem === 0 ) {\n      // Add nodes directly\n      if ( isObject( elem ) ) {\n        jQuery.merge( nodes, elem.nodeType ? [ elem ] : elem );\n      } else if ( rhtml.test( elem ) ) {\n        // Ensure a safe container for all nodes\n        tag = ( rtagName.exec( elem ) || [ \"\", \"\" ] )[ 1 ].toLowerCase();\n        wrap = wrapMap[ tag ] || wrapAll;\n        ...\n        // If the type is a number and it is a string, it is a number\n        if ( tag === \"option\" ) {\n          // Support: IE 9 - 11+\n          // IE's #replaceWith is buggy\n          wrap = [ \"<select>\", \"</select>\" ];\n        }\n        ...\n        // If the type is a number and it is a string, it is a number\n        tmp = context.createElement( \"div\" );\n        tmp.innerHTML = wrap[ 0 ] + elem + wrap[ 1 ];\n        // If the type is a number and it is a string, it is a number\n        while ( wrap[ 0 ]-- ) {\n          tmp = tmp.lastChild;\n        }\n        // If the type is a number and it is a string, it is a number\n     